course: dictionary
lesson: 9006
title: Skilfully and how things are done
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Adverbs tell us how, when or where something happens. Skilfully tells us how someone did something: in a way that shows skill and good ability, cleverly and well.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. A player who dodges two defenders and scores, or a surgeon who sews a wound with neat hands, is doing the job skilfully. The word praises doing it well, not doing it by luck.
explain: «Many blind people use sounds more skilfully, partly because the brain rewires and attention is trained; their ears themselves are not stronger.» Skilfully means using sounds cleverly and well. The clue is that the ears are not stronger, so the skill comes from training and attention.
fact: Skilfully is an adverb: it describes how an action is done.
fact: «Skilfully» means in a way that shows skill and good ability; cleverly and well.
fact: Skilfully is the UK, Australian, New Zealand and Irish spelling; in the USA it is spelt skillfully.
fact: The root word is skill: a person who is skilful is good at something, often because they have practised.
fact: Skilfully means well done, so it praises good ability rather than luck.
exercise: The striker skilfully dodged two defenders and scored. What does skilfully tell us? | Angrily, after a mistake | * In a clever, well-done way | By accident, with no skill at all | Very slowly and loudly
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in this sentence: «Her teacher said she handled the tricky question skilfully.» | teacher | tricky | * skilfully | she
exercise: Dad says, «Only a skilled chef can cut onions so skilfully.» What does skilfully mean here? | Noisily, while dropping things | * With expert, clever hands | With no care at all | Slowly and sadly
test: «Many blind people use sounds more skilfully, partly because the brain rewires and attention is trained.» Why can they use sounds more skilfully? | * Their brain is trained and rewired, and they pay close attention | They shout to hear better | Their ears are stronger than other people's | They are simply lucky
test: The surgeon sewed the wound ___, with neat and expert stitches. Which word fits best? | sadly | happily | * skilfully | noisily
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to skilfully? | Lazily | Quietly | Accidentally | * Cleverly and well
